CASE 03 — Throughput Modeling & KPI Realignment

Context
At the start of a quarter, leadership asked for hiring forecasts and KPI commitments. Sourcing expectations were aggressive — 100+ InMails per week — despite heavy inbound volume and limited hiring manager interview capacity.

One hiring manager could conduct only three interviews per week, yet sourcing pressure continued to increase.

The Problem
Targets were not anchored to operational constraints. Recruiter KPIs assumed volume productivity rather than throughput reality. Increasing sourcing would not increase hires — it would increase backlog and candidate frustration.

Design & Approach
I built a funnel model based on historical conversion rates and hiring manager interview capacity. Starting from interview availability (three per week), I reverse-engineered required recruiter screens and application volume using observed conversion rates.

Inbound application conversion averaged approximately 10%. InMail response rates averaged 20%. I calculated the necessary sourcing volume required to maintain throughput without exceeding capacity or allowing more than 50% inbound candidates in process.

I presented the model to leadership and the hiring manager to recalibrate outbound expectations and align KPIs with system capacity.

Impact
Outbound sourcing targets were reduced and aligned with operational reality. Recruiter effort shifted from excessive volume to calibrated throughput. Stakeholder expectations stabilized. Pipeline management improved without increasing candidate backlog.

Why It Matters
Talent operations is not about activity volume. It is about aligning effort with system constraints so hiring becomes predictable rather than reactive.


